a silent prayer for a life lost of his  eight children may meet kvick says his  son Ibrahim was his favorite three  months ago ebrahims body was returned  from Syria the 26 year old died while  fighting for the Kurdish resistance he  was helpful and he cared a great deal  for his people we have relatives in the  Kurdish part of Syria he wanted to take  up arms to protect them from the enemy  Ibrahim kvick lived in on Bernice on a  small Kurdish town near the  turkish-syrian border he ran a food shop  here now his older brother has taken  over the shop Ibrahim had always dreamed  of a Kurdish revolution one day he  decided to take action under the name  air doll he spent eight months fighting  for Kurdish autonomy on Syrian soil  before he left he wrote off his  customers debts put things in order and  closed the shop then he was gone none of  us knew about his plans but we respect  his decision as Kurds we are repressed  and attacked everywhere not only in  Syria but in Iraq Iran and Turkey we  have to defend ourselves metaphor that  Matthew  whether they join the ranks of Kurdish  fighters taking up arms against Islamic  state forces in Syria or the militant  Kurdish Workers Party fighting on home  soil many young Kurds see violent  conflict as their only escape from  poverty and discrimination the PKK flag  has become a common sight that the  funerals of Kurds killed in fighting in  Syria many don't differentiate between  the Turkish government and Islamic state  terrorists two years ago the Turkish  government and the PKK agreed on a  ceasefire based on the principle of  peace in return for reforms but the  Kurdish Peace and Democracy party is  disappointed with the government's  concessions we don't want a new civil  war we suffered under that for 40 years  for just allowing us to speak our own  language is not enough we want  recognition of our culture and you want  autonomy if we're to prevent our boys  from taking up arms will have to take  serious steps in that direction what are  the madam Altimus yet eq but the PKK is  calling for a return to violence across  the region and the call is being heard  close to the Syrian border we meet Ahmad  a turkish kurd the young medical student  left his hometown of adana to join the  fight for Kurdistan how and where he  doesn't yet know but he's sure that  relying on peaceful means is not the  answer the Turkish nationalists continue  to humiliate us by saying there is only  one nationality and one language the  peace talks have taken us nowhere so at  least if we join an armed struggle we  can demonstrate our pride in our roots  and it gives us a space to live in  freedom someone on the other marching  when a police car drives past Ahmad  makes a run for it by now he will be in  the mountains with the PKK it was this  dream of freedom and a better life that  drew Ibrahim kuvak to join the Kurdish  armed resistance movement before he left  he wrote a goodbye letter to his brother  there is so much injustice I refuse to  accept I don't just mean what we as  Kurds are made to suffer but also other  people and groups around the world he  lost his life for his belief in justice
 

